.TH "CSSMATHINVERT.VALUE" "3JS" "August 7, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathInvert.value \- CSSMathInvert: value property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]value\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]CSSMathInvert\f[R] interface returns the
\f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] that is being inverted.
.PP
This is the parameter that was passed to the constructor when this
object was created.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] or one of its derived types.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathInvert\f[R] object, then reads
its \f[CR]value\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] inverted = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathInvert(CSS.percent(4));

console.log(inverted.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 4, unit: \(dqpercent\(dq}\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]value\f[R] returns whatever was passed to \f[CR]arg\f[R] in the
constructor.
In this case, we passed \f[CR]CSS.percent(4)\f[R], so \f[CR]value\f[R]
is a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R].
Passing an expression such as \f[CR]CSS.percent(4).add(CSS.em(2))\f[R]
would result in \f[CR]value\f[R] returning a \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R].
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]CSSMathNegate.value\f[R]
